 "geology_interpretation": "The province (90-150 deg E, 40-70 deg N, centered ~55 N 120 E, immediately NE of the Caloris basin whose center is 31.9 N 161.9 E) is, on the disk evidence measured here, a low-reflectance-material-poor, chemically depleted, comparatively young smooth-plains terrane. Relative to the rest of 40-70 N it is strongly LRM-poor (Klima flag fraction 0.050 vs 0.195; Peplowski 0.184 vs 0.312), depleted in Mg/Si (0.359 vs 0.405), S/Si (0.060 vs 0.073) and Ca/Si (0.134 vs 0.160), slightly enriched in Al/Si (0.263 vs 0.247), and crater-poor across the 10-20 and 20-50 km bins (120 vs 244 and 68 vs 104 per 1e6 km2), while elevation, 1-deg DEM roughness and Bouguer anomaly show no significant difference. INTERPRETATION (recall, not verified on disk): this footprint plausibly coincides with the extensive northern/circum-Caloris smooth volcanic plains of Mercury. That assignment is internally consistent with the measured signature: low Mg/Si with elevated Al/Si and low S is the smooth-plains geochemistry (as opposed to the high-Mg region and intercrater plains; global XRS element maps of Nittler et al. 2020, 10.1016/j.icarus.2020.113716, and the smooth-plains distribution of Denevi et al. 2013, 10.1016/j.icarus.2012.10.008), the crater-poverty indicates a resurfaced young unit, and burial of the graphite-bearing crust by young volcanics is consistent with the LRM deficit. Exact unit boundaries and model ages are NOT verified here and should be checked against a published Mercury geologic map before use. The scientifically notable point is that this hollow-rich province is LRM-POOR, opposite to the global hollow-LRM association: when the coverage null is reweighted toward LRM the province's observed/null rises from 4.8 to 19.6 (and to 21.9 with crater-interior weighting added), so the excess is not explained by LRM material distribution or by crater-interior availability - it is anomalous with respect to both leading geological predictors. Coordinates for the PI to check against the Kinczyk et al. 2020 geologic map (reference supplied in the task brief; not independently verified in this session): box 90-150 E x 40-70 N; densest fields 55.0 N 113.2 E (n=124) and 48.9 N 132.5 E (n=116).",
 "host_crater_notes": "Two largest fields lack a host under the 1.1R rule: 55.0N 113.2E (n=124) sits at 1.15R of an 88-km crater (id 932, 53.9N 113.8E) - a near miss; 48.9N 132.5E (n=116) has no sizeable catalogued crater within range (nearest >10km crater ~87 km away), i.e. it is a plains/small-crater field. Named host craters found: Kofi (56.7N 118.2E, D 128 km), Oskison (60.4N 144.9E, D 115 km), Pasch (46.1N 134.8E, D 38 km), and Caloris (assigned to 4 SE-corner fields within 1.1R of the basin center - basin, not a normal host crater)."
